.TH ENGINE1999-BASIC 7 "October 2026" "Engine 1999 @VERSION@" "Engine1999 Manual"
.SH NAME
engine1999\-basic \- the BASIC language of Engine1999
.SH DESCRIPTION
The BASIC of
.BR engine1999 (1)
and
.BR engine1999c (1)
is a classic line\-numbered BASIC with the structure of a modern one.
Programs are tokenized, compiled to the instructions of a stack machine and run by it.
This page is a summary; the complete reference is
.I basic\-language.md
in /usr/local/share/doc/engine1999/ (docs/ in the source tree), and the whole syntax is on one page in
.IR grammar.html .
.SH PROGRAMS
.TP
.B Lines
A line with a number from 0 to 999999 is stored in the program; a line without one runs at once (direct mode).
Several statements on a line are separated by a colon.
A comment starts with an apostrophe or with
.B REM
(at the start of a statement; it is listed as an apostrophe).
Words are not case sensitive; strings keep their case.
.TP
.B Targets
GOTO, GOSUB and the other jumps take a line number or a label
.RB ( LABEL
.IR name ).
.TP
.B Files
.B SAVE "name"
writes a text listing (\fIname.bas\fR, in UTF-8: the Engine's text is ISO-8859-15, and
.BR LOAD ", " MERGE " and " CHAIN
read a listing that is well-formed UTF-8 with non-ASCII characters as UTF-8 and any other listing as ISO-8859-15; characters the set lacks become ?),
.B SAVE "name",B
the binary tokenized form (\fIname.e99\fR);
.B SAVE
alone writes the file of the last
.B LOAD
or
.B SAVE
(in the format that file has; an error if there is none);
.B LOAD "name"
finds either, and
.B MERGE
and
.B CHAIN
combine programs.
.B LIBRARY "name","prefix"
(the first statement of a library file) and
.B LOAD LIBRARY "name"
(in a program that needs a library) are declarations: a program names the libraries it needs, and
.B RUN
finds them (the current directory, \fIlib/\fR, the program's own directory, the installed libraries), compiles them ahead of the program, and runs the
.B PROC
named \fIprefix\fRINIT of each. A library cannot be run. It is typed at the prompt never. See
.IR docs/library-support.md .
The shared libraries of the demo games (util, input, hiscore, text) are in \fIdemos/lib/\fR.
.B SAVE LIBRARY "name"
writes a library as \fIname.lib\fR (with
.BR ,B :
\fIname.l99\fR);
.B LIBRARIES
lists the libraries of the last
.BR RUN ,
.B LIBRARIES "name"
the procedures, functions and labels of one.
A text file may start with a
.B #!
line.
.TP
.B TOOL
.IR name$ " [, " arg$ ]
runs a tool, a BASIC program of the tools folder (the editors
.BR spriteed ,
.BR fonted ,
.B musiced
and
.BR sfxed ),
in a state of its own and returns when it ends: the stored program, its variables and the text on the screen stay. The tool is searched as
.IR name.bas
in
.IR tools/
of the current directory, next to the program, in
.BR $ENGINE1999_HOME/tools
and in the installed
.IR lib/engine1999/tools .
The argument is what the tool reads as
.BR ARG$(1)
(a file to open). The security policy does not apply to a tool. A tool owns the screen while it runs: a program with a UI, sprites or tiles on the screen finds them gone afterwards.
Not available in the console host (and not in
.B BUILD
executables, which embed no tools).
.TP
.B Ending
.B BYE [code]
or
.B QUIT [code]
ends the program and the Engine (or the built executable) with the exit status
.I code
(0 to 255).
.B EXIT
is the loop word, not a way to quit.
.SH DATA
Numbers are floating point; an integer variable has a
.B %
suffix; a string variable a
.B $
suffix (strings are binary safe, up to 65535 bytes).
Arrays have up to six dimensions
.RB ( DIM ).
.B LET
is optional.
Operators:
.B + \- * / \e MOD **
(\fB\e\fR and
.B MOD
are integer division and remainder),
the comparisons
.BR "= <> < > <= >=" ,
.BR "AND OR XOR NOT NAND NOR XNOR IMP NIMP EQV NEQV" ,
the shifts
.BR "SHL SHR ASL ASR LSHIFT RSHIFT" ,
and
.B +
for strings.
.SH STATEMENTS
.TP
.B Output and input
PRINT, PRINT USING, AT, HTAB, VTAB, FORMAT$, CLIPBOARD, INPUT, LINE INPUT, GET, WRITE, DATA, READ, RESTORE, CLS, LOCATE, COLOR
.TP
.B Control flow
IF ... THEN ... ELSE, UNLESS, FOR ... NEXT (also DOWNTO, FOR EACH), WHILE ... WEND, REPEAT ... UNTIL, UNTIL ... UEND,
EXIT, LEAVE, ITERATE, AGAIN, GOTO, GOSUB, RETURN, ON ... GOTO/GOSUB, POP, END
.TP
.B Procedures and functions
PROC ... END PROC and CALL, FUNC ... END FUNC, DEF FN, LOCAL, RETURN value, EXIT PROC
.TP
.B Errors
ON ERROR GOTO/GOSUB/OFF (or GOTO 0), RESUME, ERROR n, and the functions ERR$, ERR and ERL
.TP
.B Break key
ESC, Ctrl+C and (window) PAUSE/BREAK stop a running program; BREAK OFF makes ESC an ordinary key (INKEY$, GET) for the program, BREAK ON gives it its meaning back. Ctrl+C always stops, also in executables made with BUILD.
.TP
.B Keys held down
KEY$("EVENT") returns the oldest key event that has not been read ("+A" went down, "\-A" was let go, "" none), and ON KEY GOSUB/GOTO/OFF runs a handler when a key goes down or is let go (window only, as the console host cannot tell when a key is let go). KEY("name") is \-1 while the key is held down and 0 otherwise ("A", "SPACE", "LEFT", "F1", "SHIFT", "ANY", ...), for any number of keys at once; unlike INKEY$ it knows when a key is let go. It works in the window only: the console host (and console executables) give the error "KEY is not available in this host", because a terminal never reports that a key is let go.
.TP
.B Game controllers
JOY(name$ [, player]) reads the game controllers (up to four, with one standard layout whatever the brand: buttons "A" "B" "X" "Y" "LB" "RB" "BACK" "START" "GUIDE" "LS" "RS" "UP" "DOWN" "LEFT" "RIGHT" "SHARE" give -1 or 0, the axes "LX" "LY" "RX" "RY" -1..1 with up negative and "LT" "RT" 0..1; also "CONNECTED", "COUNT", "BUTTONS", "PRESSED", "RELEASED", "EVENTS", "DEADZONE"); a player without a pad reads 0. JOY$("NAME" [, player]) is the pad's name. JOY DEADZONE d sets the dead zone of the sticks (0..0.9). ON JOY GOSUB/GOTO/OFF runs a handler when a button is pressed or released or a pad is plugged in or out. The console host has no controllers (JOY("COUNT") is 0).
.TP
.B The mouse
SPRITE n,PATH a$ gives a sprite a path that the engine runs by itself, one step per frame, like a PLAY string runs per note: X+3*20 moves 3 pixels a frame for 20 frames, X=n and Y=n set a coordinate, M dx,dy moves both ways, W n waits n frames, A a,len,speed plays an animation, D i shows an image, H and S hide and show, P n sets the priority, T n sets the default duration of a move, [ ... ]n repeats n times, R starts again and E n reports an event; SPRITE n,PATH "" stops it, SPRITE("PATH",n) tells whether one runs. ON SPRITE GOSUB/GOTO/OFF runs a handler when a path has reported an event, which the handler reads with SPRITE("EVENT") (the event number, -1 when none; SPRITE("EVENTSPR") is its sprite) until it returns -1. See docs/graphics-subsystem.md.
.PP
MOUSE SHOW, HIDE, POS x,y, SHAPE name and TIMEOUT seconds control the mouse pointer, which is shown for 3 seconds after the mouse moves, a button is pressed or one of SHOW, POS, SHAPE is executed and then hidden so that the text can be read (TIMEOUT 0: it stays until HIDE). The shapes are ARROW, WAIT, BUSY, TEXT, HAND, GRAB, CROSS, SIZEH, SIZEV, MOVE, NO and HELP. The functions MOUSE("X") and MOUSE("Y") (0..639, 0..299), MOUSE("B") (buttons held: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle), MOUSE("PRESSED") and MOUSE("RELEASED") (buttons since the last call), MOUSE("WHEEL"), MOUSE("SHAPE") and MOUSE("VISIBLE") read it. ON MOUSE GOSUB/GOTO/OFF runs a handler when a button is pressed or released or the wheel turns (not on motion; highest priority of the event handlers). Works in the window and in the console host's screen mode (a terminal that reports the mouse: the pointer is the cell under it with swapped colors and its shape is the terminal's pointer, OSC 22; a cell is 8 x 12 pixels); elsewhere the console host gives an error.
.PP
UI OPEN starts the UI subsystem (window only): widgets drawn on the graphics layer, which the UI owns while it is open. UI PANEL, LABEL, BUTTON, CHECK, RADIO, TEXT, LIST, SLIDER, SCROLL (a scroll bar; UI PAGE sets the size of its knob) and PROGRESS create widgets (id, x, y, w, h and a text; ids are chosen by the program), UI CAPTION, VALUE, RANGE, ENABLE, SHOW, FOCUS, ITEM, CLEARLIST, REMOVE, MODAL, POPUP, GETTEXT and ITEMTEXT change and read them. UI UPDATE, once a frame, takes the mouse and the keys, draws what changed and queues events; UI("EVENT") is the id of the next event (0 = none), UI("KIND") and UI("VALUE") describe it (1 click, 2 change, 3 select, 4 double click, 5 Return in a text field, 6 cancel), UI("GET",id), UI("COUNT",id), UI("FOCUS"), UI("OPEN") and UI("EVENTS") read state. ON UI GOSUB/GOTO/OFF runs a handler whenever a widget has queued an event; the handler reads the events with UI("EVENT") until it returns 0. UI CANVAS id,x,y,w,h,cw,ch makes a zoomed grid of cw x ch cells of palette indices (UI PIXEL id,x,y,c and UI("PIXEL",id,x,y) set and read a cell, UI IMAGE id,a$ and UI GETIMAGE id,a$ load and save all of them as a string); a press or drag on a cell queues the event kind 7 (left button) or 8 (right button) with the value y*cw+x. UI WINDOW id,x,y,w,h,title$ makes a panel that can be dragged by its title bar, comes to the front when it is clicked and has a close box (the cancel event of its id); the widgets created inside it move with it; UI MOVE id,x,y and UI RAISE id do the same from the program. While the UI is open a game controller drives the focus (D-pad or left stick: arrows, A presses, B is Escape, LB and RB are Shift+Tab and Tab); UI JOY 0 switches that off. UI COLOR id,fg[,bg] sets the colors of one widget (palette entries, -1 = the theme's), UI THEME n loads a theme (0 classic, 1 dark, 2 high contrast), and UI SYNTAX id,1 colors the text of an editor (UI EDIT) as BASIC source like LIST does (UI SYNTAXCOLOR slot,c changes a color; UI HILITE id,a$ lets a program give the classes of other languages). UI CLOSE ends it. See docs/ui-subsystem.md.
.PP
W3D is the software 3D sector renderer (window; docs/3d-subsystem.md): a world in the style of Doom and Duke Nukem 3D, drawn into a view on the graphics layer that is GFX TARGET. A world is made of sectors with a floor and a ceiling height, a floor and a ceiling picture and a light level, bounded by walls; a wall with a sector behind it is a portal (doorways, stairs, windows, lifts). W3D TEXTURE n,a$ takes a picture in the GFX GRAB format (made with GFX statements or GFX LOAD and GFX GRAB; palette index 0 is see-through in things and masked walls). W3D SECTOR s,floor,ceil[,floortex[,ceiltex[,light]]] defines a sector (ceiltex \-1 is the sky picture set by W3D SKY), W3D WALL s,x1,y1,x2,y2,tex[,next] adds a wall (list them counterclockwise; next is the sector behind it, \-1 a solid wall); W3D WALLTEX, WALLOFF, WALLFLAG (1 see\-through picture, 2 movement blocked, 4 shots and sight blocked), HEIGHT (moves floor and ceiling: doors and lifts), LIGHT and TAG change walls and sectors. W3D CAMERA x,y,angle[,pitch[,lift]] puts the viewer into the world (angles in degrees, 0 is along x), W3D TURN turns it, W3D MOVE forward,right walks with collision, sliding along walls, up steps and through portals it fits through (W3D PLAYER radius,height,eye,step; W3D LIFT h raises the feet for a jump: steps up to the step height plus h can be taken, W3D("LIFT") reads it). W3D VIEW x,y,w,h[,scale[,fov]] sets where the picture goes on the layer (scale 2 draws every pixel twice: faster), W3D FOG a distance of darkening, W3D COLORMAP builds the brightness table from the palette (call it after the palette is set up; palette ramps of one color make good shading), W3D RENDER draws the picture. W3D THING n,x,y,tex,w,h places a picture that always faces the viewer (THINGZ, THINGTEX, THINGOFF, THINGLIGHT; THINGMOVE dx,dy walks it like W3D MOVE). W3D("X"), ("Y"), ("Z"), ("ANGLE"), ("PITCH"), ("SECTOR") and ("HIT") read the viewer, W3D("SECTORAT",x,y), ("FLOORAT",x,y), ("FLOOR",s), ("CEIL",s), ("LIGHT",s), ("TAG",s), ("WALLS",s) the sectors, W3D("TX",n), ("TY",n), ("TZ",n), ("TSECTOR",n), ("TON",n), ("DIST",n) and ("BEARING",n) the things, W3D("SHOOT",range) the thing a shot along the view direction hits (with ("SHOTKIND"), ("SHOTDIST"), ("SHOTX"), ("SHOTY")), W3D("USE",reach) the sector behind the portal wall in front of the viewer, W3D("SEES",n) whether a thing has a line of sight to the viewer. The console host runs all of this except the picture (W3D RENDER and W3D COLORMAP do nothing there), so a game\(aqs logic can be tested headless. See docs/3d-subsystem.md.
.TP
.B Timers
AFTER, EVERY, WAIT, FRAME [n] (wait for the next n screen refreshes, 1 by default; in step with the display, no processor time), and the function REMAIN
.TP
.B Files and devices
OPEN, CLOSE, PRINT #, INPUT #, LINE INPUT #, GET #, SEEK, FIELD, LSET, RSET, GETREC, PUTREC, KILL, RENAME, MKDIR, CHDIR, ADDRESS, FILES
.TP
.B Sound
SOUND, PLAY, SFX, WAVE, TEMPO, PITCH, VOLUME, ENVELOPE, VIBRATO, TREMOLO. There are 16 channels: 1 to 8 for music (PLAY voice n plays on channel n), 9 to 16 for sound effects (SFX without a channel takes the highest free one of them).
.TP
.B Graphics
GFX, TXT, TILE, SPRITE and DESIGN, with their own sub\-words (layers, palettes, drawing, tiles, sprites with collisions and sprite groups, BMP picture files for layers, sprites, tiles and palettes (LOAD, SAVE, PALLOAD, PALSAVE), text areas), and ON COLLISION. A program has the sprites 0 to 247; 248 to 255 belong to the engine (the mouse pointer is 255) and give "Illegal argument".
.TP
.B Program commands
LIST, LOAD, SAVE, RUN, NEW, CLEAR, DELETE, RENUM, MERGE, CHAIN, AUTO, EDIT, EDIT WINDOW, MONITOR, STOP, CONT, TRACE, BUILD, BYE, QUIT
.TP
.B Other
DIM, ERASE, RANDOMIZE, DEG, RAD, GRAD, POKE, SYMBOL
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B Numbers
INT, FIX, FLOOR, CEIL, ABS, SGN, SQR, SIN, COS, TAN, ATN, ASN, ACS, SINH, COSH, TANH, ASINH, ACOSH, ATANH, ROUND, CINT, CDBL, EXP, LOG, LOG2, LOG10, RND, PI, VAL, ASC, LEN, CVI, CVL, CVS, CVD, PEEK, TI, REMAIN, PLAYING,
EOF, LOF, LOC, FRE, ARGC, RGB, POINT, ERL, ERR, KEY, MIN, MAX, ATAN2
.TP
.B Strings
CHR$, STR$, LEFT$, RIGHT$, MID$, BIN$, QUA$, OCT$, DEC$, HEX$, INPUT$, INKEY$, MKI$, MKL$, MKS$, MKD$, TIME$, DATE$, DATETIME$, PEEK$, ARG$, ENV$, ERR$, ADDRESS$, SPACE$, STRING$
.SH PROGRAM ARGUMENTS AND THE ENVIRONMENT
.B ARGC
is the number of program arguments,
.B ARG$(n)
the n\-th (0 is the program), and
.B ENV$(name$)
an environment variable.
Arguments come from the command line of
.BR engine1999c (1)
and of built executables.
.SH FILES AND DEVICES
A file name may start with a driver and a colon.
.TP
.B file:
Disk files (the default).
.TP
.B mem:
A RAM disk.
.TP
.B null:
Swallows output, reads nothing.
.TP
.B sh:
A shell command as a file:
.BR "OPEN ""sh:ls -l"" FOR INPUT AS #1" .
.TP
.B res:
Read\-only game assets in the directory
.IR res/ .
.TP
.B config: local:
A program's own files. The name is the running program's: the base name of its file without the extension (and without a leading
.IR engine1999\- ,
so the installed game
.B engine1999\-rustinpeace
and
.B rustinpeace.bas
share their files).
.B config:
is the folder
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/<name>/
(default
.IR ~/.config/<name>/ ),
.B local:
is
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/<name>/
(default
.IR ~/.local/share/<name>/ ).
The folder is created when it is needed (mode 0700). A name can not leave it: absolute names, a
.B ..
component and symbolic links are
.BR "Permission denied" ;
sub\-folders are made with MKDIR; CHDIR is not offered. All files below a program's two folders together are limited to 64 MiB
.RB ( "Disk full" ).
Nothing is asked for: a program only reaches its own folders. A program without a usable name (typed in at the prompt, or named
.BR engine1999 ,
whose folder holds the permission store) does not get an error: the name is used as an ordinary file name, as if there were no prefix.
.TP
.B dir:
A directory as a read\-only text file: the first line is its full name, then the sub\-directories (with a
.BR / ),
then the files, in alphabetical order. A pattern such as
.I dir:/tmp/*.bas;*.txt
selects files.
.TP
.B con:
The screen and keyboard as a channel.
.TP
.B play:
Lines written are music strings.
.TP
.B tcp:
A network connection (client).
.TP
.BR stdin: ", " stdout: ", " stderr:
The standard streams of the process.
.SH SECURITY
While a program runs it may use the folder it was loaded from (the folder of the executable for one made with
.BR BUILD ;
the current directory for a program that was typed in) and nothing else without permission. Commands typed at the prompt are never restricted.
.PP
Without permission a program cannot read or write files outside its folder (a path with
.B ..
or an absolute path elsewhere; symbolic links are followed), run a shell command (a shell literal or
.BR OPEN\ "sh:..." ),
or open a network connection
.RB ( tcp: ).
The error is
.BR "Permission denied" .
.B mem: null: con: stdin: stdout: stderr: res: config: local:
and
.B play:
need no permission.
.PP
When a program needs more, the window shows a dialog ("The running program wants to: ... Allow it?") and after an Allow asks for how long: this session, 24 hours or always. The console host asks on the terminal (a key: n, s, d, a); with no terminal the answer is no. What is allowed for 24 hours or always is stored in
.IR ~/.config/engine1999/grants
(or the file named by
.BR ENGINE1999_GRANTS ),
keyed by the path of the program and a hash of its content, so a changed program has no consent.
.PP
A program can ask for permissions up front with a file next to it,
.I NAME.perm
(NAME is the program file without its extension), with lines
.B request read
(also
.BR write ", " shell ", " net ", " all ;
# starts a comment): the user is asked once when the program starts. The options
.B \-\-allow=list
and
.B \-\-deny=list
(for an executable made with BUILD:
.B \-\-engine1999\-allow=list
and
.BR \-\-engine1999\-deny=list )
decide without asking. See docs/security-policy.md.
.SH BUILDING EXECUTABLES
.B BUILD "name" [, WINDOW | CONSOLE]
makes a standalone executable from the program in memory, using only the system linker
.BR ld (1).
A
.B WINDOW
executable has the runtime of
.BR engine1999 (1)
and needs libSDL2 to run; a
.B CONSOLE
executable has the runtime of
.BR engine1999c (1)
and needs only the C and the math library.
Without the option the kind of the host is made.
Names that end in
.IR .bas ,
.IR .e99 ,
.I .lib
or
.I .l99
are refused.
A program that names libraries (\fBLOAD LIBRARY\fR) is built together with them: the executable carries the libraries
and never looks for their files.
A missing or wrong library stops the build.
.PP
.B BUILD
needs the runtime kit, the files \fIengine1999.o\fR, \fIengine1999c.o\fR and \fIengine.lib\fR.
It looks for them, in this order, in the directory of the running program,
.IR ../lib/engine1999
beside it,
.BR $ENGINE1999_HOME ,
the current directory,
.IR /usr/local/lib/engine1999 ,
.I /usr/lib/engine1999
and
.IR /usr/local/share/engine1999 ;
the linker, the startup files and the libraries it finds by itself.
.B make install
puts the kit into
.IR /usr/local/lib/engine1999 ,
the shared libraries into
.IR /usr/local/lib/engine1999/lib
and the tools (\fBTOOL\fR) into
.IR /usr/local/lib/engine1999/tools .
.PP
The executable starts the program at once.
The command line is the program's (\fBARGC\fR, \fBARG$\fR), except
.B \-\-engine1999\-about
as the first argument, which prints the license notice of the runtime.
A console executable also takes
.BR \-\-engine1999\-screen ,
.B \-\-engine1999\-line
and
.B \-\-engine1999\-no\-dump
(first, before the program's words; see
.BR engine1999c (1)).
.PP
The runtime in an executable is covered by the Engine1999 Runtime Exception: the executable may be distributed under terms of the author's
choice if the runtime in it is the unmodified one and its notice stays.
See LICENSE\-EXCEPTION.
